## §3. Numerical Boundaries — Risk R3 Mitigation

**This section is the primary deliverable of M0.** Every operation in §2 gets an
explicit dtype contract. This table is the hard-fix — neither M2/M3 Triton
implementation nor any future optimization may demote any fp32 boundary listed
here without a spec revision and architect sign-off.

**Default rule:** fp32 for any reduction or accumulation; bf16 only for HBM I/O
and broadcasted multiplications. When in doubt: fp32.

| Op | Input dtype | Compute dtype | Output dtype | Justification |
|----|-------------|---------------|--------------|---------------|
| `u_Q = Q / ‖Q‖₂` (norm) | bf16 (Q) | **fp32** (norm + division) | **fp32** (u_Q) | bf16 norm has ~12% tie rate at D=64 (Paper 1 §B.2, §C.1); fp32 required throughout dispatch chain |
| `K_tile · u_Q` (projection score) | bf16 × fp32 | **fp32** accumulator (dot product) | bf16 (heap key) | K promoted to fp32 in projection einsum; back to bf16 only as top-K comparison key — precision loss here is tolerable (selection criterion, not softmax) |
| top-K heap comparison | bf16 scores, int32 indices | bf16 compare | int32 indices | Tie-break: stable ordering, smaller original index wins; deterministic across runs |
| `Q[b,h,:].to(fp32) · K_sel` (attention scores) | bf16 × bf16 → fp32 | **fp32** accumulator | fp32 logits | Standard FlashAttention pattern; never demote to bf16 between K multiply and softmax |
| running max `m` | fp32 logits | **fp32** | fp32 | **Critical R3 boundary:** bf16 max state breaks online softmax at long context; see quantitative analysis below |
| running denominator `l` | fp32 | **fp32** | fp32 | Same — bf16 sum loses relative accuracy; see analysis below |
| `exp(scores − m_new)` | fp32 | **fp32** | fp32 | bf16 exp has 1-bit effective mantissa in many ranges; unusable for softmax normalization |
| running output `o` | fp32 | **fp32** | fp32 | Same reasoning as `l`; never touch bf16 before final divide |
| `o / l` (final normalization) | fp32 | fp32 | **bf16** | Single demotion at kernel output boundary — all numerical work complete |
| scale `1/sqrt(D)` | — | fp32 constant | fp32 | Compile-time constant; no casting error |

### §3.1 Three additional invariants

1. **Q is promoted to fp32 once** (Step 1 for u_Q, Step 4 for attention scores). Do
   not re-promote from bf16 per tile — this would re-introduce the original bf16
   rounding error on each promotion.

2. **Online softmax state (m, l, o) never touches bf16**, even in the spill-to-HBM
   path (Option B pass boundary; see §4.3). When passes exchange state, they exchange
   fp32 values.

3. **K and V are bf16 in HBM** (storage format only). They are always promoted to
   fp32 in SRAM before any arithmetic. No bf16 arithmetic in the kernel hot path.

### §3.2 Quantitative justification for fp32 running state

At N = 20K with c_floor = 0.5, k_eff = 10000. The inner softmax accumulates over
10000 terms in `l` and `o`.

**bf16 running sum error bound:**
bf16 has 7-bit mantissa → machine epsilon ε₇ = 2⁻⁷ ≈ 0.0078.
For a sum of k terms, worst-case accumulation error ≈ O(√k · ε₇).
At k = 10000: error ≈ √10000 · 2⁻⁷ = 100 · 0.0078 ≈ **0.78 relative error**.
This is catastrophic — would dominate the +0.31% PPL budget we observed in H.1.B.

**fp32 running sum error bound:**
fp32 has 23-bit mantissa → machine epsilon ε₂₃ = 2⁻²³ ≈ 1.2×10⁻⁷.
At k = 10000: error ≈ √10000 · 2⁻²³ = 100 · 1.2×10⁻⁷ ≈ **1.2×10⁻⁵ relative error**.
Three orders of magnitude below the PPL budget. fp32 is sufficient.

**Conclusion:** bf16 running softmax state is completely unusable at k_eff ≥ 1000.
fp32 is the only viable option.

