## §5. Equivalence Claim

This section defines what M5 validation verifies. Three tiers. No bitwise-identical
claim (impossible with bf16 non-associative reductions).

Tier 1 (numerical equivalence) is split into §5.1.1 (intermediate tensors —
Pass 1 scores, Pass 2 indices) and §5.1.2 (final output O), reflecting the
distinct noise sources at each stage of the pipeline.

### Tier 1 — Numerical equivalence

Different kernels in the pipeline produce different tensor types with different
sources of numerical noise. Tier 1 thresholds are split per tensor type to
calibrate against the actual physical minimum for each.

#### §5.1.1 Intermediate tensor tolerance (Pass 1 scores, Pass 2 indices)

For **Pass 1 scores** `[B, H, N]` bf16, comparing M2 Triton output vs M1 reference:

```
max |scores_triton - scores_ref|_∞  ≤  1.0e-2    (≤ 2 bf16 ULP at projection magnitude)
mean (scores_triton - scores_ref)²   ≤  1e-6     (RMSE² floor)
```

**Justification.** bf16 ULP at magnitude ~1.0 is exactly 0.0078125 (= 7.8e-3).
Different reduction orders between cuBLAS (M1's `torch.einsum`) and Triton
(`tl.sum` element-wise) produce results that differ by ≤1 ULP for identical
fp32 input. This is the physical minimum given non-associative bf16 arithmetic;
algorithmic equivalence cannot be tightened further without locking reduction
order across kernels (which would defeat Triton's parallelism).

The 2-ULP threshold (1.0e-2) provides headroom for cumulative cross-tile
variance while still being tight enough to catch genuine algorithmic divergence.

For **Pass 2 indices** `[B, H, k_eff]` int32:

```
exact_match(indices_triton, indices_ref) ≥ 99.9% of (b, h) tuples
```

Note: indices are integer; "tolerance" applies as exact-match fraction, not
distance metric. The 0.1% allowance covers bf16 ties at window boundary
where stable tie-break might differ across reduction orders.

#### §5.1.2 Final output tolerance (Pass 3 / full kernel output O)

For **final output O** `[B, H, D]` bf16, comparing fused-pipeline Triton output
vs M1 end-to-end reference:

```
max |O_triton - O_ref|_∞  ≤  5e-3      (original threshold, unchanged)
mean (O_triton - O_ref)²   ≤  1e-6     (RMSE² floor)
```

**Justification.** Final output O has been through softmax + attention-weighted
sum, which **dampens** intermediate score noise: a 1-ULP perturbation in scores
produces a sub-ULP perturbation in softmax probabilities (because softmax is
local-Lipschitz with constant ≤1 in logit space), which produces a vanishing
perturbation in the weighted V sum. Therefore the 5e-3 threshold (~⅔ ULP at
output magnitude) is achievable for the fused pipeline output even though
intermediate scores tolerance is 2 ULPs.

The asymmetry between §5.1.1 (intermediate) and §5.1.2 (output) is expected
and correct: intermediate tensors exhibit reduction-order noise; final output
exhibits softmax-dampened noise.

#### Threshold application by milestone

| Milestone | Output tested | Tier 1 threshold |
|-----------|---------------|------------------|
| M2 Pass 1 (projection) | scores `[B, H, N]` | §5.1.1 (≤ 1.0e-2 max, ≤ 1e-6 RMSE²) |
| M2 Pass 2 (top-K) | indices `[B, H, k_eff]` | §5.1.1 (≥ 99.9% exact match) |
| M3 Pass 3 (fused attention) | O `[B, H, D]` | §5.1.2 (≤ 5e-3 max, ≤ 1e-6 RMSE²) |
| M5 end-to-end validation | O `[B, H, D]` | §5.1.2 |

Thresholds calibrated to bf16 cumulative noise per Paper 1 §A.3 cross-validation.

### Tier 2 — Statistical PPL equivalence

End-to-end PPL on N = 5000 WikiText-2 trace (Llama-3.2-1B):

```
|PPL_triton - PPL_python_ref| / PPL_python_ref  ≤ 5×10⁻⁴   (0.05%)
```

Comparison baseline is **M1 Python top-K reference** (this spec §8), not the
current Python rank-window implementation. The architectural improvement (top-K
vs rank-window) accounts for ~0.1–0.2% PPL difference; that is the claimed gain,
not a bug to be nulled out.

### Tier 3 — Algorithmic invariants

For each `(b, h, decode_step)` tuple over a representative N=5000 trace:

```
Jaccard(S_triton, S_ref) = 1.0   for ≥ 99.9% of tuples      (index set agreement)
cos(u_Q_triton, u_Q_ref) ≥ 0.9999                            (Q-axis alignment)
|S_triton| = k_eff   exactly                                  (no off-by-one in selection size)
```

The 0.1% index disagreement tolerance covers bf16 score ties at the k_eff boundary
(two keys with identical bf16 score; either is valid). Ties are resolved by smaller
index (§6), but the Triton implementation may encounter reordering due to parallel
heap updates — the 0.1% budget accommodates this.

### Bitwise equality: explicitly NOT claimed

bf16 reduction order is non-associative; CUDA Tensor Core schedules vary across
kernel launches; parallel heap merges in Triton are non-deterministic within a warp.
Bitwise equality would require disabling Tensor Cores (~3× perf regression) and
sequential warp reduction, negating the purpose of the kernel. Claims 1–3 above
are sufficient for production deployment.

